body:
What follows is an example that deterministically trigger this race condition in the spec, in Gecko.

```html
<!doctype html>
<script src="/resources/testharness.js"></script>
<script src="/resources/testharnessreport.js"></script>
<body>
<iframe src="/common/blank.html" id="child"></iframe>
<script>

promise_test(async t => {
  await new Promise(r => child.onload = r);
  await new Promise(r => t.step_timeout(r, 0));

  const nav = child.contentWindow.navigation;
  const history = child.contentWindow.history;

  // Build entries: [initial, #1, #2], current at #2.
  await nav.navigate("#1").finished;
  await nav.navigate("#2").finished;

  // Go back so #2 is a forward entry we can target then destroy.
  await nav.back().finished;

  const targetKey = nav.entries()[2].key;
  const result = nav.traverseTo(targetKey);
  await new Promise(r => t.step_timeout(r, 0));
  // pushState truncates entry #2 and can trigger the race condition in the spec
  history.pushState({}, "", "#pushed");

  const committedResult = await result.committed.then(
    v => ({ settled: "fulfilled", value: v }),
    e => ({ settled: "rejected", error: e })
  );
  const finishedResult = await result.finished.then(
    v => ({ settled: "fulfilled", value: v }),
    e => ({ settled: "rejected", error: e })
  );

  assert_equals(committedResult.settled, "rejected",
                "traverseTo().committed should reject");
  assert_equals(committedResult.error.name, "AbortError",
                "traverseTo().committed rejects with AbortError");

  assert_equals(finishedResult.settled, "rejected",
                "traverseTo().finished should reject");
  assert_equals(finishedResult.error.name, "AbortError",
                "traverseTo().finished rejects with AbortError");
}, "traverseTo() aborts when pushState truncates target entry after IPC dispatch (same-origin iframe)");
</script>
</body>

```

Chrome passes this.

[URL and history update steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#url-and-history-update-steps) in step 11 calls [update the navigation API entries for a same-document navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation) is called via the line `history.pushState({}, "", "#pushed")`

It's harder to tell if chrome doesn't have the spec assertion step and aborts, or if they are not hitting the race condition (Session History Traversal Queue and the Event Loop are truly parallel).

But I think this seems to suggest that, [the spec assertion](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#update-the-navigation-api-entries-for-a-same-document-navigation:~:text=navigation%2E-,Assert%3A%20navigation%27s%20current%20entry%20index%20is%20not%20%E2%88%921) should be re-written as an [abort the ongoing navigation](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#abort-the-ongoing-navigation) instead. I think that this is what chrome does, given that steps [12.2 and 12.3](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#performing-a-navigation-api-traversal) both reject with `InvalidStateError` and not `AbortError`, yet chrome is indeed throwing `AbortError`, the default value for aborting an ongoing navigation.

Pinging @noamr || @farre with this new info.
